<!-- Axol on the Ox cart. GENERATED by scripts/build_jelly_urdfs.py from the
     Onshape export; do not edit by hand. Arm links, joints and joint frames
     are the classic axol.urdf's, so FK and gravity compensation match it
     exactly; joint limits and meshes are the export's. One model for IK,
     the viewers and physics simulators. Moving: the 14 arm joints, the
     cart's lift (lift, 0 = fully raised, positive lowers), each gripper's
     fingers (left_finger_1 / right_finger_1, the second finger mimics; 0 =
     closed, 0.0588 m = open). With every body joint at zero the torso root
     (link root) is the world origin, so poses match the classic axol.urdf.
     IK (almond_axol.kinematics.model) freezes every joint it does not solve
     (arm IK: all but the arms; whole-body IK: the body joints too) and
     collides capsule-friendly pieces, the custom ik_collision elements,
     instead of each part's collision hull; importers ignore them. IK
     freezes the lift fully raised (its CAD pose), where the cart's caster
     frame sits below anything the arms can reach, and keeps the arms clear
     of the shoulder bar (s1), the head-camera mount (head) and the lift
     column (base). Lowering the lift brings the frame up toward the hands,
     which IK does not see. Physics: arm joints carry AxolConfig's measured
     friction as <dynamics friction> (Coulomb, N·m) and damping (viscous,
     N·m·s/rad), per arm, and the motors' impedance gains as <drive
     stiffness> (kp, N·m/rad) and damping (kd, N·m·s/rad): a custom element
     no importer reads, so set them as each joint's position-drive gains
     after import. Arm masses and CoMs are AxolConfig's tuned gravity-comp
     values (the wrist-3 body is split so the gripper, fingers and wrist
     camera carry their share while the assembly's total mass and CoM stay
     as tuned); every other mass is an ESTIMATE (Body.masses in the
     generator) - replace with measured values. Inertia tensors assume
     uniform density over each part's convex hull. Effort/velocity: arm
     joints keep axol.urdf's values; the rest are estimates. Camera frames
     (*_optical) use the ROS optical convention (+z forward, +x right, +y
     down); an Isaac Sim camera attached with ROS camera axes needs no extra
     rotation. Mesh paths are relative to this file. Wrist camera frames
     (*_wrist_camera_optical, ZED X One S fisheye): the origin is the
     projection centre from Stereolabs' model of the camera (its FOV solid's
     rays meet on the lens axis 2.2 mm behind the front glass); fitting real
     wrist images through each camera's factory calibration agrees within
     the fit's ~3 mm resolution. Roll puts the gripper at the bottom of the
     image, as in real frames. Head camera frames (head_camera_*_optical,
     ZED X Mini Wide): each lens's projection centre from Stereolabs' model,
     7.2 mm behind its front cover.
-->
